    </div>
  </div>
  <nav class="catrow"><span class="catgrp"><b>News</b><a href="/category/national">national</a><a href="/category/world">world</a><a href="/category/defence">defence</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Money</b><a href="/category/business">business</a><a href="/category/finance">finance</a><a href="/category/education">education</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Sports</b><a href="/category/sports">sports</a><a href="/category/cricket">cricket</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Culture</b><a href="/category/entertainment">entertainment</a><a href="/category/positive">positive</a><a href="/category/funny">funny</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Life</b><a href="/category/science">science</a><a href="/category/environment">environment</a><a href="/category/space">space</a><a href="/category/health">health</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Tech</b><a href="/category/tech">tech</a><a href="/category/ai">ai</a></span></nav></div><div class="gtr"><span>Read in your language:</span><div id="google_translate_element"></div></div><div class="wrap"><main id="content"><p class="crumb"><a href="/">← Front page</a> · <a href="/archive">Archive</a> · <a href="/category/sports">sports</a></p>
     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · BBC Sport · 2026-10-09 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Fury-Joshua Fight Tickets Sell Out in One Hour for Cardiff Bout</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Boxing</span> <span>#Tyson Fury</span> <span>#Anthony Joshua</span> <span>#Sports</span></div>
       <p>Tickets for the highly anticipated heavyweight boxing match between Tyson Fury and Anthony Joshua at Cardiff's Principality Stadium sold out in just one hour, according to BBC Sport. The rapid sale underscores the immense public interest in the all-British contest.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-09/fury-joshua-fight-tickets-sell-out-in-one-hour-for-cardiff-b.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Approximately 80,000 tickets went on sale at 12:00 BST on Friday. Within minutes of the sale commencing, over 500,000 people were reported to be waiting in the online queue. Ticket prices ranged from £88.50 for the lowest tier to £13,128, including booking fees, for the highest-priced seats.</p><p>Following the quick sell-out, tickets swiftly appeared on resale websites, with ringside VIP seats advertised for as much as £73,645, and upper-tier seats available for £425. This indicates a high demand beyond the initial supply.</p><p>The event is scheduled for a bill that begins on 11 December, with Fury and Joshua expected to enter the ring in the early hours of 12 December. Ringwalks are anticipated around 01:00 GMT on Saturday, 12 December, following a brief live music performance. The stadium is set to remain open until 03:30 GMT.</p><p>The fight is being promoted as a collaboration between Saudi boxing powerbroker Turki Alalshikh's The Ring and UFC chief Dana White, and will be broadcast globally on Netflix. Organisers are yet to confirm the undercard fights for the event.</p><p>Both Joshua, 36, and Fury, 38, are former two-time world champions. They signed contracts for the bout earlier this year, though the date and venue, which had considered London's Wembley Stadium and New York's Madison Square Garden, were only recently confirmed for Cardiff. The Principality Stadium has a history of hosting late-night boxing, including Joe Calzaghe's fight with Mikkel Kessler in November 2007, which also began at 01:00 GMT, accommodating US broadcast schedules.</p><p>Just 11 days prior to the ticket sale, uncertainty surrounded the fight's occurrence due to a dispute over UFC chief Dana White's promotional role. Joshua's promoter Eddie Hearn had stated his fighter's contract prevented White from having such a role. The British rivals eventually met at a news conference on 30 September to officially kick off the build-up to what is considered one of the most anticipated fights in British history. Reports indicate that Fury and Joshua are set to share a purse worth close to $200 million (£151 million).</p><p>What to watch: The undercard announcements and final preparations for the eagerly awaited December bout.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Detailed report that successfully incorporates the specific ticket pricing, queue numbers, and background context provided in the full text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/boxing/articles/cwp9gxzyplk4o?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Sport</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
