       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · ET Healthworld · 2026-10-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>IIT BHU Scientists Develop Green Light-Activated Technology for Bacterial Infections</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#IIT BHU</span> <span>#Green Light Technology</span> <span>#Bacterial Infections</span> <span>#Antimicrobial Resistance</span></div>
       <p>Scientists at IIT BHU have developed a novel green light-activated technology aimed at combating bacterial infections. Their research paper, titled &quot;Green-light-activated Os(II) metallophotoantibiotics for antibacterial therapy and infected wound healing,&quot; outlines this promising scientific approach.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-09/iit-bhu-scientists-develop-green-light-activated-technology-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The technology offers a potential solution to significant challenges in modern healthcare, specifically targeting antimicrobial resistance, persistent bacterial infections, and the healing of infected wounds. This development marks a step forward in addressing these critical health concerns.</p><p>What to watch: Further research and clinical trials for this technology.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a concise and accurate representation of the provided summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/medical-devices/iit-bhu-scientists-develop-novel-green-light-activated-technology-to-fight-bacterial-infections/134812330" rel="noopener">ET Healthworld</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
