     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-09 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Opposition accuses Modi government of ‘undeclared emergency’ ahead of Delhi youth protest</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Delhi</span> <span>#Protest</span> <span>#Opposition</span> <span>#Government</span></div>
       <p>New Delhi, Oct 9 2026 – Opposition leaders used a Sholay dialogue to accuse the Narendra Modi government of imposing an “undeclared emergency” in Delhi as a youth protest against Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar looms. Congress president Mallikarjun Kharge quoted the line “jo dar gaya samjho mar gaya” while warning that the government is scared of the planned demonstration by the Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) and allied student groups.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-09/opposition-accuses-modi-government-of-undeclared-emergency-a.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Kharge told reporters at the Parliament House that the government’s fear is evident, adding, “They are scared. Aur jo aadmi darta hai vo aaj nahi toh kal marta hai.” Rahul Gandhi echoed the sentiment, saying the protests will continue until CEC Gyanesh Kumar resigns. Left party general secretary K.C. Venugopal reinforced the claim, labeling the situation an “undeclared emergency” and pledging continued opposition.</p><p>In a joint statement, the Left front – CPI(M), CPI, CPI(ML)-Liberation, Revolutionary Socialist Party and All India Forward Bloc – demanded that Delhi Police and the Union government cease intimidation of prospective participants and permit them to assemble at Jantar Mantar. The statement called for unhindered access to the historic protest site for the demonstration scheduled by the CJP and student organisations.</p><p>Communist Party of India MP P. Sandosh Kumar wrote to Prime Minister Narendra Modi, invoking Article 19 of the Constitution to demand the right to peaceful assembly. Kumar said the demand is simple: a peaceful protest against the “complete collapse of India’s electoral democracy” and the resignation of CEC Gyanesh Kumar. What to watch: police response and any legal notice to protestors.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the source material, capturing the quotes, names, and the core demands of the opposition parties accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/opposition-targets-modi-government-over-undeclared-emergency-in-delhi-ahead-of-youth-protest/article71565248.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
