     <article class="story single" data-beat="entertainment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENTERTAINMENT</span> · Bollywood Hungama · 2026-10-09 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Saira Banu Pays Emotional Tribute to Kathak Guru Roshan Kumari Ji, Recalls Lifetime Bond</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Saira Banu</span> <span>#Roshan Kumari Ji</span> <span>#Kathak</span> <span>#Bollywood</span></div>
       <p>Veteran actor Saira Banu has penned an emotional tribute to her Kathak teacher, Roshan Kumari Ji, remembering the late classical dancer as a mentor, confidante, and family member. In a heartfelt social media post, Banu reflected on their profound relationship, which began when she was a young student learning Kathak and evolved into a deeply personal bond over the years.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-09/saira-banu-pays-emotional-tribute-to-kathak-guru-roshan-kuma.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Banu highlighted Roshan Kumari Ji’s significant contributions to classical dance, noting her iconic performance in Satyajit Ray’s Jalsaghar. She described the nearly ten-minute Kathak sequence in the film as one of Indian cinema's most magnificent moments, where Roshan Ji “didn’t merely occupy the frame she owned it with her charisma.” Banu emphasized that watching Roshan Ji dance made “the entire room come alive around her.”</p><p>Sharing her thoughts, Saira Banu wrote, “Some goodbyes are impossible to write. Today, I find myself trying to say goodbye to my beloved Guru, Roshan Kumari Ji, and every word feels inadequate.” She further elaborated that Roshan Ji became much more than a teacher, transforming into “a confidante, a friend, an elder sister-like presence, and eventually, family.” Banu recounted that she was a young girl when she began learning Kathak, unaware that she was meeting someone who would leave an “imprint on my soul forever.”</p><p>Roshan Kumari Ji, a true legend of the Jaipur Gharana, taught Banu that dance encompassed more than just movement; it was “discipline, devotion, expression and, above all, a way of revealing the heart.” Banu recalled her teacher's extraordinary artistry, noting her powerful footwork that could “thunder” and mesmerising “chakkars,” always performed with “unmistakable grace and dignity.”</p><p>The bond between them was so strong that Roshan Kumari Ji was present at Saira Banu's wedding to actor Dilip Kumar. Banu described having her Guru present on such a “precious day” as one of the memories she will “hold close to my heart forever.” This presence underscored the deep personal connection that extended beyond formal dance lessons.</p><p>Beyond the stage, Banu remembered Roshan Kumari Ji's personal elegance. “Oh, how she loved her sarees! She loved dressing up, looking beautiful, and carrying herself with that effortless elegance that was so uniquely hers.” Banu noted that Roshan Ji could “walk into a room without saying a word and somehow become its centre,” adding that her brilliance was “never loud” and she “didn’t need to announce that she was a legend” because “Her art had already said it all.”</p><p>Banu expressed feeling “deeply blessed” to have been Roshan Kumari Ji's student, crediting her teacher for whatever understanding she gained of Kathak's grace and discipline. She reflected that Roshan Ji's “love and teaching” were woven into her ability to imbibe the dance form. However, Banu cherished most “not what she taught my feet, but what she taught my heart.”</p><p>Concluding her heartfelt tribute, Saira Banu expressed her reluctance to say goodbye, preferring the words “phir milenge” (we will meet again), believing that “some bonds are not broken by death. They simply become memories we carry more carefully.” She wished for Roshan Kumari Ji’s “ghungroos [to] keep ringing somewhere beyond this world” and hoped to “always remain worthy of having once been called your student.” Saira Banu, associated with Hindi cinema since the 1960s and known for films like Junglee, Padosan, and Hera Pheri, emphasized that her teacher's influence went far beyond artistic training.</p><p>What to watch: Satyajit Ray’s Jalsaghar for Roshan Kumari Ji’s Kathak sequence.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: A thorough and well-written tribute that captures the emotional tone and specific biographical details provided in the source.</p>
