     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-09 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Teacher Develops Portable Chemistry Lab for Students</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Abhilash Thiruvoth, a chemistry teacher at Government Vocational Higher Secondary School for Girls in Nadakkavu, has developed a portable lab called 'Rasappetti' to improve student engagement. Designed to overcome time constraints in 40-minute class periods, the wooden box allows teachers to demonstrate experiments directly in the classroom.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-09/teacher-develops-portable-chemistry-lab-for-students.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The kit contains essential equipment like test tubes, beakers, and various chemical reagents. Teachers for classes 6 through 10 can customize the contents based on their specific syllabus requirements, with supplies lasting approximately two months per load. The project was created to make science learning more practical and accessible for high school students.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate summary of the 'Rasappetti' project. It captures the purpose, design, and teacher's intent effectively.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/kerala/how-a-keralam-teachers-portable-chemistry-lab-is-sparking-students-interest-in-experiments/article71558644.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
